  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Thursday: Strong high pressure ridging extending\nup through the Intermountain West from the south-central Plains\nwill keep the region mainly dry and increasingly hot for much \nthis week. 850mb temps climbing into the mid and upper 20s (C)\nwill cause the heat to likely peak in the Wed/Thu timeframe. Widespread\nmoderate HeatRisk with pockets of major HeatRisk is expected as\nearly as Tuesday, though by midweek the major HeatRisk area will\nexpand to encompass much of the Spokane/Coeur d'Alene area and\nwest across the Columbia Basin and Waterville Plateau. There \nstill remains some uncertainty in how high cloud cover and \nwildfire smoke will impact temps, though ensemble guidance \ncontinues to indicate high confidence in max temps exceeding the\nupper 90s or 100s over a sizable footprint of this region; NBM \nprobabilities for MaxT>=100F max out at around 60%-90% \nWednesday. As such, heat headlines will eventually be needed \nonce we get a bit closer and the finer details come into focus. \nThe aforementioned cloud cover will be in place as monsoonal \nmoisture slowly advects into the region from the south this \nweek. This moisture plume will likely make it into southern WA \nand central ID, but will begin to encounter northerly mid level \nflow coming down from British Columbia and Alberta. Early in the\nweek, in additional to some added clouds, there could be a few \nsprinkles/virga across Palouse and areas further south Monday \nthough model soundings indicate plenty of dry air beneath 12kft \nso not expecting any wetting rains. Later in the week, a Pacific\ntrough will attempt to slide into the Northwest as the ridge \npotentially breaks down. Will need to continue monitoring trends\nas this added lifting mechanism may lead to increasing chances \nfor convection Wednesday into Thursday.\n\nFriday through Sunday: Ensemble guidance spread remains\nunusually low in the general evolution of the longwave pattern \nnext weekend. A trough pivoting southeastward from Alaska will \nphase with a shortwave off the Pacific NW coast, with the \naggregate trough sliding northeastward towards the crest of the \nstrong high pressure ridge across the desert southwest. This \nwill result in some relief from the heat seen earlier in the \nweek, though the ridge weakening may lead to elevated fire \nweather conditions with decreasing humidity and breezy winds.\nLong range ensembles do indicate decreasing PWAT anomalies which\nsignals a decreased threat for thunderstorms, though can't quite\nrule out some isolated convection from Thursday rolling to \nFriday.",
